# On/off variables in this file (BEHIND_CLOUDFLARE, AUTOBAN, AUTO_REBOOT,
# HOSTED, …) are read as BOOLEANS: only 1 / true / yes / on (any case) turn
# them on. `KEY=0` — the natural way to write "off" — is off, and so is any
# other value. The app and oikonome.sh read them the same way, so the
# generated proxy config can never disagree with the running app.
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=change-me
OIKONOME_APP_PASSWORD=change-me-too
# The RUNTIME admin role (oikonome_admin — cross-tenant reads,
# tenant lifecycle, no DDL). app/worker carry this instead of the postgres
# superuser; only the one-shot migrate container uses POSTGRES_PASSWORD.
# install.sh generates it (and backfills older installs).
OIKONOME_ADMIN_PASSWORD=change-me-three
# Job-queue (Redis) AUTH. install.sh generates it (and backfills older
# installs). Empty = no AUTH — the pre-AUTH behavior; the queue is
# compose-network-internal either way.
REDIS_PASSWORD=
# Isolates this install's containers, network, volumes AND image tag —
# without it, two installs on one host would build the same shared
# image tag and clobber each other on upgrade. install.sh appends it
# (COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME=oikonome-<port>); set it yourself on a manual
# install.
# REQUIRED — encrypts bank tokens, webhook secrets + TOTP seeds at rest;
# compose refuses to start without it (like the passwords above). Generate:
#   openssl rand -base64 32 | tr '+/' '-_'
# (or: python -m oikonome.db.crypto). install.sh generates it for you.
OIKONOME_MASTER_KEY=
# Public origin of this install (e.g. https://money.example.com). Emailed
# links (password reset) are ONLY sent when this is set — the app never
# builds an emailed link from request headers. With SMTP on but this
# unset, the reset link is written to the server log instead.
# The daily email links back here too (its header, and the one-tap
# "Needs you" buttons), but only when this is https on a real domain —
# a bare IP or a .lan/.local name gets the email without links.
# Optional for LAN/localhost installs.
# OIKONOME_BASE_URL=
# Reverse-proxy addresses (comma-separated IPs or CIDRs) whose
# X-Forwarded-For / X-Forwarded-Proto headers are trusted — set this when
# running behind a proxy so rate limiting and the sessions panel see real
# client IPs, and HTTPS at the proxy enables HSTS + Secure cookies.
# Empty (default) = both headers are ignored.
# OIKONOME_TRUSTED_PROXIES=127.0.0.1,10.89.0.0/24
# Behind Cloudflare (orange-cloud): set OIKONOME_BEHIND_CLOUDFLARE=1 and re-run
# `./oikonome.sh https` — the generated Caddyfile then trusts Cloudflare's edge
# ranges and reads the real visitor from Cf-Connecting-Ip, so client_ip still
# resolves the real client. Spoof-safe: a direct-origin forger (peer not in a
# CF range) only attributes their own IP.
# The CF ranges are baked into oikonome.sh; refresh them if CF changes the list
# (cloudflare.com/ips). This is separate from OIKONOME_TRUSTED_PROXIES above.
# OIKONOME_BEHIND_CLOUDFLARE=1
# Auto-ban (abuse defense): a client IP that keeps tripping rate limits or
# failing login is short-blocked BEFORE routing (bots get 429'd cheaply). ON
# by default; set to 0 to disable. Only public IPs are ever banned — loopback
# and LAN are exempt — and the block self-expires (15 min, escalating for
# repeat offenders). Keyed on the RESOLVED client IP, so it works behind a
# proxy / Cloudflare when OIKONOME_TRUSTED_PROXIES (or BEHIND_CLOUDFLARE) is set.
# OIKONOME_AUTOBAN=0
# Every block/limit/auth-failure is logged as a single line
# `oikonome-security event=<kind> ip=<resolved-client-ip> ...` so you can point
# fail2ban (or any log tail) at it — note `ip=` is the real client past the
# proxy, so a host jail bans the actual offender, not the proxy/Cloudflare edge.
#
# Reject breached passwords at signup / reset via Have I Been Pwned's
# k-anonymity range API (only the first 5 chars of the SHA-1 ever leave the
# host — never the password). OFF by default (no external call); fail-open, so
# a HIBP outage degrades to the length rule rather than blocking the door.
# OIKONOME_PWNED_CHECK=1
#
# Cloudflare Turnstile — a "are you human" check on /login and /signup. It
# answers what rate limits cannot: a credential-stuffing run spread across a
# botnet stays under every per-IP limit, but still has to solve the challenge.
# OFF unless BOTH keys are set, so a self-hosted instance never loads the
# third-party script and needs no Cloudflare account. Free plan is enough
# (20 widgets, 10 hostnames each, unlimited challenges). Get keys at
# dash.cloudflare.com → Turnstile → Add widget.
# Sign-in is challenged only once attempts start FAILING against that IP or
# account (signup is challenged every time). A blocked script renders no
# widget to click, so an unconditional check would lock out anyone running an
# ad blocker or a filtering DNS; risk-gating keeps that dead end reachable
# only by someone already getting their password wrong. See docs/reverse-proxy.md.
# Fail-open: if Cloudflare is unreachable the login still works — an outage
# there must not lock you out of your own finances.
# OIKONOME_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY=
# OIKONOME_TURNSTILE_SECRET=
# One-time gate for the first-boot setup wizard: while no account exists,
# /setup only answers to /setup?token=<this>. install.sh generates it and
# prints the full link; ./oikonome.sh status reprints it until claimed.
# Both print it only to a terminal: a scripted run (stdout captured, or
# OIKONOME_ASSUME_YES set) is told to run status interactively instead.
# Empty = wizard open to anyone who can reach the port (pre-token behavior).
OIKONOME_SETUP_TOKEN=
# daily email DELIVERY (any SMTP provider) — recipients and schedule are
# configured in the web UI under Settings. Also carries password-reset
# links and the 💬 Feedback tab's reports; with no SMTP set, feedback
# falls back to a downloadable .zip.
OIKONOME_SMTP_HOST=
# 587 is the standard submission port. Many cloud hosts block outbound
# 25/465/587 by default — if mail silently stops, use 2525, which most
# relays also accept. The worker probes the relay every half hour and the
# admin console / Doctor show a red banner when the path is dead.
OIKONOME_SMTP_PORT=587
OIKONOME_SMTP_USER=
OIKONOME_SMTP_PASSWORD=
# The From address. Left unset, app and compose both fall back to
# oikonome@localhost — set this to a mailbox your relay is actually allowed
# to send as. It ships COMMENTED OUT on purpose: an uncommented example
# address makes a copied .env send as a reserved domain, which many relays
# reject outright.
# OIKONOME_SMTP_FROM=you@yourdomain.tld
# STARTTLS certificates are verified; set to 1 only for a relay with a
# self-signed certificate (e.g. Proton Mail Bridge on localhost)
# OIKONOME_SMTP_NO_VERIFY=1
# STARTTLS is used when the relay offers it (default on). Set to 0 only
# for a relay that requires a plaintext session. Every on/off knob in this
# file reads 1/true/yes/on as ON and 0/false/no/off (or empty) as OFF.
# These OIKONOME_SMTP_* vars are the FALLBACK: they apply while the SMTP
# host in Settings → Email delivery is empty. Once you configure SMTP in
# the app, that config wins, including its own "Use STARTTLS" checkbox.
# OIKONOME_SMTP_STARTTLS=1
# where 💬 Feedback reports are emailed when SMTP is configured
# OIKONOME_FEEDBACK_TO=feedback@example.com
# hosted only: operator notices go to this address — one mail per new
# household (the signup address and its household id; the reclaim door
# sends a marked one too). Sent off the request, so a dead relay never
# fails a signup; leave it unset and no notice is sent at all.
# OIKONOME_OPERATOR_EMAIL=you@example.com
# Addresses whose signups do NOT raise an operator notice — comma-separated
# shell globs matched against the whole address — for an automated signup
# test that mints a household on every run and would otherwise mail you
# about a person who does not exist. Empty mutes nothing.
# OIKONOME_OPERATOR_NOTICE_SKIP=you+test*@example.com,*@test.invalid
# hosted only: self-service signup. Unset, hosted signup requires an
# operator-minted invite bound to the address; set to 1 anyone may create a
# household, which is the general-availability posture. An invite is still
# honoured whenever one is presented — this switch only decides whether
# signing up WITHOUT one is allowed. Self-host ignores it: that instance
# bootstraps through the token-gated /setup wizard and stays one household.
# OIKONOME_OPEN_SIGNUP=1
# Where a locked-out or turned-away person is told to write — on the
# sign-in, recovery and reset pages and in the recovery mails. Unset, the
# app names no address anywhere.
# OIKONOME_SUPPORT_EMAIL=you@example.com
# Your own site and privacy policy, if you publish them: the sign-in page
# links "No account?" at the site when signup is closed, and adds a
# Privacy link. Unset, neither link renders.
# OIKONOME_SITE_URL=https://example.org
# OIKONOME_PRIVACY_URL=https://example.org/privacy
# Spam defense for public email intake (signup). ON for
# hosted by default; set to 1 on self-host to opt in (fetches public
# blocklists), or 0 to force off.
# OIKONOME_SPAM_DEFENSE=1
# The live StopForumSpam lookup portion. Set to off to use the downloaded
# blocklists only (no per-submission external call). Budget + cache TTL:
# OIKONOME_SPAM_API=off
# OIKONOME_SPAM_API_BUDGET_MS=400
# OIKONOME_SPAM_CACHE_TTL_DAYS=7
# extra domains never blocklisted (comma-separated), appended to the built-in
# major-provider safelist:
# OIKONOME_SPAM_SAFELIST_EXTRA=example.edu,mycorp.com
# hosted only: operator admin console at /admin/console (tenant list +
# health, invite management, audit-logged actions). Unset = console off
# (every route 404s). Generate: openssl rand -hex 32. Optionally restrict
# by client address with OIKONOME_ADMIN_IPS (comma-separated CIDRs,
# honored through OIKONOME_TRUSTED_PROXIES like rate limiting).
# OIKONOME_ADMIN_TOKEN=
# OIKONOME_ADMIN_IPS=100.64.0.0/10
# The token above is BREAK-GLASS. The real console credential is an
# operator passkey, enrolled from the console itself (Ops → Operator access)
# — hardware-bound, unphishable, and it puts a name on every audit row.
# Enrol a key, then set this to 1 and restart: the token alone stops minting
# sessions (keep a copy of it offline anyway — a plain-http LAN install has
# no secure context and cannot enrol a passkey at all, so there the token
# stays the only way in).
# LOST THE KEY? One command on the host, no .env edit and no restart:
#   docker compose exec -T app oikonome admin-keys --reset
# It removes every enrolled key, and this flag is ignored once none remain,
# so the token signs in again immediately. `admin-keys` on its own lists
# them; `--remove <id>` drops one.
# OIKONOME_ADMIN_REQUIRE_PASSKEY=1
#
# HOSTED INSTANCES ARE PASSKEY-ONLY, ALWAYS. With OIKONOME_HOSTED set, the
# operator token can never sign in — a hosted box has a domain and a cert,
# so it can always enrol a passkey, and a shared secret there is only a
# phishing target. A token left in a hosted .env is inert: it still switches
# the console ON, but it cannot open it. Self-host keeps the token, because
# a plain-http LAN install has no secure context and can enrol nothing.
#
# NO TOKEN AT ALL — the tidy hosted configuration. Leave
# OIKONOME_ADMIN_TOKEN unset/empty and set this instead: the console is on,
# and a passkey is the ONLY credential that exists (an empty token is
# refused, never "matched"). There is then nothing to phish, nothing to
# leak, and nothing sitting in plaintext on the host.
# Bootstrap and recovery are the SAME one command, on the host:
#   docker compose exec -T app oikonome admin-enrol
# It prints a single-use link (15 min) that enrols one key without signing
# in — mintable only from inside the container, i.e. only by someone who
# already controls the box. Use it to enrol the first key, and again if you
# ever lose one (then drop the stale key with `admin-keys --remove <id>`).
# OIKONOME_ADMIN_ENABLED=1
# Additional origins the console may sign you in at, comma-separated.
# A phone cannot use /etc/hosts, so the way to reach a tailnet-only console
# from one is a Tailscale MagicDNS name — `tailscale serve` on the host gives
# it a real certificate, and adding that origin here lets a passkey be
# enrolled against it. WebAuthn binds a credential to ONE relying party, so
# each origin needs its own key. Deliberately an allowlist and not the Host
# header: rp_id decides which credentials a browser offers.
# OIKONOME_ADMIN_ORIGINS=https://myhost.tailXXXX.ts.net:8443
#
# Automated maintenance reboot. When the host reports a pending reboot
# (kernel/libc update), the nightly job runs the SAME choreography as the
# console's reboot button: a countdown broadcast to anyone signed in, a 60s
# deadline, then the ops-command flag the root watcher picks up (which adds
# its own 60s grace). A no-op on any night the host has nothing pending, so
# most nights it does nothing at all. Needs the host-agent watcher installed
# (self-host: it is not part of a plain compose install); off unless set.
# OIKONOME_AUTO_REBOOT=1
#
# If Cloudflare Access fronts /admin*, verify its assertion HERE too
# — an edge policy binds nothing if the origin is reachable directly. Set
# both (team = your Access org, aud = the application's Audience tag) and any
# request without a valid, unexpired, correctly-audienced Access JWT gets the
# same 404 as a probe. Fails closed, including if Cloudflare is unreachable:
# to get back in, unset these and restart.
# OIKONOME_CF_ACCESS_TEAM=yourteam.cloudflareaccess.com
# OIKONOME_CF_ACCESS_AUD=
# When an operator schedules a tenant deletion from the console
# it freezes immediately and the worker wipes it after this many days
# (restorable until then). 0 = no grace (delete is immediate). The console
# also offers an explicit "delete now" for the abuse case regardless.
# OIKONOME_DELETE_GRACE_DAYS=7

# Hosted only: signups nobody ever confirmed. Day 7 (when the first
# link expires) each unconfirmed person gets one reminder with a fresh
# link; after this many days the household is frozen (a final mail names
# the date and carries a fresh link — clicking it reopens the account)
# and the ordinary purge erases it a week later. A household with a
# subscription, or where anyone ever confirmed, is never touched.
# 0 = off (reminders included).
# OIKONOME_UNVERIFIED_REAP_DAYS=30

# EXTERNAL / managed Postgres (DO managed PG, RDS, your own server)
# instead of the bundled container: see docker/compose.external-db.yaml
# for the full variable list, then activate with:
# COMPOSE_FILE=compose.yaml:compose.external-db.yaml
# DB connection pools (per process): size tenant + control pools so their
# sum, plus the worker and the provider's own agents, fits your server's
# connection cap (budget worked out in server/oikonome/db/tenancy.py).
# Defaults suit a 25-conn managed plan; raise only with the plan's cap.
# OIKONOME_POOL_MAX=10                 # tenant-scoped pool (per process)
# OIKONOME_CONTROL_POOL_MAX=4          # control-plane pool (per process)
# OIKONOME_CONTROL_POOL_TIMEOUT=5      # seconds before a starved control
#   pool sheds the request with a 503 instead of queueing
# OIKONOME_TENANT_POOL_TIMEOUT=5       # same fast-fail for the tenant pool
#   (a starved tenant pool 503s in ~5 s rather than hanging ~30 s)

# Where the database lives. Leave unset for the default: ./data/postgres
# inside the install folder, so code + config + data are one directory and
# deleting the folder removes everything. Set an ABSOLUTE path to keep the
# data somewhere else.
# OIKONOME_DATA_DIR=/home/you/oikonome-data

# Host port the app is served on (container-internal stays 8080)
OIKONOME_PORT=8042

# Host ports the built-in TLS proxy (`./oikonome.sh https <domain>`) binds.
# Change them when 80/443 are already taken on this host (another proxy, a
# second Oikonome instance). Certificates still need the domain reachable
# on 80/443, so forward 80/443 → these ports at your router/firewall.
# OIKONOME_HTTP_PORT=80
# OIKONOME_HTTPS_PORT=443

# Optional: OpenAI-compatible LLM for merchant auto-categorization.
# Easiest path is the bundled local model — `./oikonome.sh llm on` (or accept
# the install prompt) starts an internal Ollama container and sets URL/MODEL
# below for you. Point these at any OpenAI-compatible endpoint to BYO instead.
# These env vars are the instance-wide DEFAULT backend; Settings → AI can
# add more backends (local or cloud) and route each task — assistant,
# categorization, receipts — to a different one, without touching this file.
# Local merchant categorizer: joblib artifact mounted at /models
# (compose binds ./models read-only). Unset = off. Confidence
# floor below which it abstains: OIKONOME_CATEGORIZER_MIN_CONF
# (default 0.55).
#OIKONOME_CATEGORIZER_MODEL=/models/categorizer.joblib
#OIKONOME_CATEGORIZER_MIN_CONF=
# the nightly-retrained per-household overlay (learns from your
# corrections; stored in the database) holds a stricter bar because its
# answers become durable rules; default 0.70
#OIKONOME_CATEGORIZER_OVERLAY_MIN_CONF=
OIKONOME_LLM_URL=
OIKONOME_LLM_MODEL=
OIKONOME_LLM_API_KEY=
OIKONOME_LLM_EXTRA_BODY=
# Bundled Ollama context window (tokens). 8192 fits a receipt photo's
# image tokens + the parse prompt; raise if you use bigger prompts.
OLLAMA_CONTEXT_LENGTH=8192
# Bundled Ollama container memory cap. `llm on` / `llm vision` size this to
# the model automatically (1.5b→3600m, 7b/vl:3b→6g, vl:7b→10g); override if
# a parse gets OOM-killed (symptom: receipt upload fails, ollama logs show
# "unexpected EOF").
# OLLAMA_MEM_LIMIT=3600m

# Hosted-only: platform Plaid — the "Connect your accounts" bank-link door
# Self-hosted installs leave these empty and use per-user BYO keys
# in the Settings card instead. IMPORTANT: set OIKONOME_PLAID_ENV=sandbox
# while using sandbox keys, or they will hit production and every link fails.
# OIKONOME_PLAID_CLIENT_ID=
# OIKONOME_PLAID_SECRET=
# OIKONOME_PLAID_ENV=production         # sandbox | development | production
# OIKONOME_PLAID_PRODUCTS_EXTRA=none   # comma list of extra billed Plaid
#   products (liabilities, investments). ONE answer for two questions: what
#   a new link asks the bank's consent for, and what the daily sync pulls.
#   They must agree — a product the sync requests without a link consenting
#   to it answers ADDITIONAL_CONSENT_REQUIRED forever, and the Accounts page
#   then reports "card due dates not shared" on every card.
#   SELF-HOST DEFAULT (variable left commented out, or left empty): BOTH —
#   your Plaid keys, your Plaid bill, and card due dates and holdings are
#   what they buy.
#   To opt out, set it to `none` (`OIKONOME_PLAID_PRODUCTS_EXTRA=none`): no
#   consent asked, nothing pulled, nothing billed beyond transactions.
#   An EMPTY value is NOT the opt-out — compose hands the container every
#   variable it declares, so an empty value is exactly what commenting this
#   line out looks like from inside, and the two cannot mean opposite
#   things. If you opted out with an empty value before, write `none`.
#   HOSTED: default off; in hosted mode an installed add-on may answer
#   instead, and this variable is then ignored.
# OIKONOME_PLAID_WEBHOOK_URL=          # public https URL of this install's
#   /api/plaid/webhook; set it and new/re-linked Items get webhook-driven
#   sync on top of the hourly poll. Empty = polling only.
# OIKONOME_PLAID_REAP_DAYS=30          # zombie-Item reaper: a connection
#   failing unrecoverably (login expired / consent revoked) for this many
#   consecutive days is auto-removed at Plaid (an Item bills monthly for
#   as long as it exists) and archived locally; the user gets a reconnect
#   notice on the Accounts page + in the daily email. 0 = never reap.
# OIKONOME_SYNC_BUCKETS=12             # aggregator poll cadence, and the one
#   lever on total aggregator traffic: the sweep runs every 5 minutes and
#   serves one bucket of tenants, so buckets × 5 minutes IS each tenant's
#   poll interval — 12 is hourly, 24 is two-hourly and halves the calls.
#   Raise it as the fleet grows; the poll is a safety net under the
#   webhooks, and its only real cost is balance/holdings freshness.

# Transactional email relays (Postmark, SES, Mailgun, …) are configured
# through the OIKONOME_SMTP_* settings above — the relay's server token goes
# in as BOTH the SMTP username and password, and the verified sender in
# OIKONOME_SMTP_FROM. There is no separate API sending path.

# Knowing when your mail STOPS arriving.
#
# A mistyped address (…@gmaill.com) bounces once, the relay then suppresses
# it, and every later send is refused before it leaves. Without the two knobs
# below the app never learns any of that: the scheduled email simply stops
# arriving and nothing anywhere says so.
#
# Set a long random value here and give the same value to your relay as the
# bounce-webhook URL, e.g. in Postmark → Servers → Webhooks:
#   https://YOURHOST/api/email/webhook?token=THE_VALUE
# (HTTP Basic in the URL works too — the secret may be either half.)
# UNSET ⇒ the endpoint 404s and nothing is exposed. A self-host install with
# a local relay has no webhook to receive and needs none of this; the send
# path still records a permanently-refused address on its own.
# OIKONOME_EMAIL_WEBHOOK_TOKEN=
#
# Only needed if your relay is Postmark but your SMTP username is NOT the
# Postmark server token (the usual setup, above, already supplies it). It
# lets a re-verified address be REACTIVATED on Postmark's suppression list —
# without it a corrected address stays dead upstream no matter what the app
# thinks. Read by server/oikonome/notify/delivery.py.
# OIKONOME_POSTMARK_TOKEN=

# Public demo instance: closes signup/password doors and makes
# the worker reset the demo household to pristine every hour. Leave unset
# on any real install.
# OIKONOME_DEMO=1

# SMS summaries — optional. All three must be set or the SMS
# channel simply doesn't exist (the Settings toggle explains why). Browser
# push notifications need no configuration: the instance generates its own
# keypair on first use.
# OIKONOME_TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID=
# OIKONOME_TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN=
# OIKONOME_TWILIO_FROM=                # +1… number or MG… messaging service

# Mobile-app push — optional, OFF by default. Phones can only be pushed
# through Apple/Google's services with the app publisher's credentials, so
# pushes route through the Oikonome push relay. Opting in tells the relay
# your instance exists; what it receives per push is {device push token,
# event kind, badge} plus, for the daily push only, the one-line verdict
# ("OVER budget by $120 · …") that the lock screen shows — never a name,
# merchant or balance (the app wakes and fetches from YOUR server). Both
# phone platforms are carried: Android through FCM, iOS through APNs. See
# docs/faq.md.
# OIKONOME_PUSH_RELAY_URL=https://push.oikonome.com
# Set to 1 to keep pushes routing-only (fixed "Today's verdict is ready"
# text; nothing about your money ever reaches the relay or the platforms).
# OIKONOME_PUSH_CONTENT_FREE=
# The relay's instance key, sent with every push. A relay that has keys
# configured refuses pushes without one; the operator of the relay you
# point at issues it. Leave unset for a relay you run yourself with no
# keys (OIKONOME_RELAY_KEYS unset on the relay side = open).
# OIKONOME_PUSH_RELAY_KEY=
# "♥ Support development" link in the app footer. Default: shown on
# self-host (→ https://oikonome.com/donate), hidden when OIKONOME_HOSTED is set.
# Set a URL to force-show it; set "off" to hide it entirely.
# OIKONOME_DONATE_URL=

# ---- tuning knobs, all optional -------------------------------------------
# IANA timezone (e.g. America/Denver) the nightly worker and notification
# send-hours run in, for every household that has not picked its own under
# Settings → Email (a household's zone wins over this). install.sh
# auto-seeds this from the host; a manual install defaults to UTC unless set.
# OIKONOME_TZ=Etc/UTC
#
# Receipt image optimization. The optimizer decodes a photo, deskews it and
# stores a cleaned copy alongside the untouched original; these bound what it
# will take on so a burst of huge photos cannot exhaust memory. Exceeding a
# limit SKIPS optimization for that upload — the original is always kept and
# an upload never fails because of these.
#   MAX_OPTIMIZE_PX        pixels above which the photo is stored as-is
#   OPTIMIZE_WORK_PX       working resolution for the deskew/warp pass
#   OPTIMIZE_CONCURRENCY   how many full-res decodes may run at once
# OIKONOME_RECEIPT_MAX_OPTIMIZE_PX=12000000
# OIKONOME_RECEIPT_OPTIMIZE_WORK_PX=2400
# OIKONOME_RECEIPT_OPTIMIZE_CONCURRENCY=3
#
# Receipt PARSING (the reader that turns a photo into a total and line
# items) has the same bound: how many full-resolution decodes may run at
# once, and how long a parse waits for a slot before it is marked failed
# and picked up by the next sweep. Defaults suit a 768 MB container.
# OIKONOME_RECEIPT_PARSE_CONCURRENCY=2
# OIKONOME_RECEIPT_PARSE_WAIT_S=120
#
# Assistant questions in flight at once, instance-wide; further asks are
# refused with a 503 the page reports as an error to retry. Each ask holds
# a worker thread for up to a few minutes on a slow local model.
# OIKONOME_ASSISTANT_CONCURRENCY=3
#
# Plaid webhook key-fetch defences. An unknown signing key id is cached as
# unknown for NEG_TTL seconds so a spray of novel ids cannot make us re-fetch,
# and FETCH_CEILING bounds concurrent outbound key fetches under the
# instance's credentials.
# OIKONOME_PLAID_WEBHOOK_NEG_TTL=60
# OIKONOME_PLAID_WEBHOOK_FETCH_CEILING=4
#
# The native apps this server trusts for passkey sign-in, as the platforms
# name them — the build YOU distribute (the mobile tree ships placeholder
# ids). Unset, the server publishes empty trust statements and accepts no
# app origin, which is right for an instance with no app of its own.
#   OIKONOME_ANDROID_PACKAGE — the Android application id
#   OIKONOME_ANDROID_CERT_SHA256 — comma-separated colon-hex SHA-256 signing
#     certificate fingerprints (public identifiers, not secrets): every key
#     the app may be signed with, e.g. a store's app-signing key and the
#     upload key used for sideloads
#   OIKONOME_IOS_APP_IDS — comma-separated "<Team ID>.<bundle id>"
# OIKONOME_ANDROID_PACKAGE=org.example.oikonome
# OIKONOME_ANDROID_CERT_SHA256=
# OIKONOME_IOS_APP_IDS=
# Extra Android packages this server trusts for passkey sign-in, comma
# separated. Android's Credential Manager checks the calling app's package
# AND its signing certificate against /.well-known/assetlinks.json before it
# will offer a passkey, so a build that renames the application id — a
# side-by-side dev variant, say — is refused even when it is signed with the
# same key, and the phone only says "sign in another way". Empty by default:
# naming another app here is a real trust grant, so a production host should
# have to say it deliberately.
# OIKONOME_ANDROID_EXTRA_PACKAGES=org.example.oikonome.dev
